 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Decision Log
     2	
     3	Decisions made during product discovery (2026-08-08 interview). Each entry: decision, and why. Locked decisions are reopened deliberately, not drifted away from — an agent that finds one inconvenient should surface the conflict, not silently work around it.
     4	
     5	## Product
     6	
     7	| # | Decision | Why |
     8	|---|---|---|
     9	| D1 | Layered history fallback: this machine → same model elsewhere → exercise, labeled | Strict machine-only starts every new machine cold; exercise-first reduces the differentiator to a tag. Fallback keeps honesty *and* usefulness. |
    10	| D2 | Machine/gym optional; remembered per gym per exercise. (Refined by D19: equipment context lives on the entry, not per set) | Free weights, home workouts, and lazy logging must work; friction kills fast logging. |
    11	| D3 | Seed equipment models only; gyms/machines always user-created | A worldwide gym database is impossible offline and its machine inventories are unknowable. Creating a gym takes 10 seconds, once. |
    12	| D4 | Catalog depth: major brands, popular lines — **1877 models across 23 manufacturers** as shipped by ticket 20 (amended 2026-08-10; the original "~hundreds" was a placeholder written before the research existed). Depth is bounded by *verifiability*, not by a count: a model name ships only if it is traceable to the manufacturer's own listing or an authorised dealer carrying the manufacturer's SKU codes (`docs/catalog-sources/`), and a line whose members are not enumerated is not seeded at all. One real machine gets exactly one catalog UUID — near-duplicate identities are a generator error, not a curation preference | Covers most commercial gyms; long tail via user entry. A plausible-but-invented model is worse than an absent one, and a *duplicated* one is worse still: D23 keys history and records on the model UUID, so two rows for one machine split the user's own history (codex-review-4). The original rationale ("weeks of work for two people") was stale on both counts — the project is solo (D5) and the work was done by an agent in one session. |
    13	| D5 | Audience: the developer alone (v1) | Cuts onboarding/dedup/polish scope. Stable IDs + export keep public release open. (Amended 2026-08-08: project is solo, not two-person.) |
    14	| D6 | Templates are generic; resolved to last-used machine per gym at workout start | One "Push Day" works at every gym; gym-pinned templates break when traveling. |
    15	| D7 | Models map to exercises; free weights are equipment tags, not machines | Machine pick auto-fills exercise (speed); multi-exercise stations prompt; barbells aren't machines. |
    16	| D8 | PRs: per-rep-count bests + est. 1RM at machine/model/exercise layers | Heaviest-set-only is meaningless for machine training; mirrors the fallback layers. |
    17	| D9 | Mixed units display as entered + marked convert toggle | Converting silently (90.7 kg for a 200 lb entry) is exactly the falseness the app rejects. |
    18	| D10 | Log-time context snapshots; archive instead of delete; model corrections prompt past-vs-future | History must survive renames, re-modeling, and deletions without silently changing meaning. |
    19	| D11 | Prefill from same-machine history only | Prefilling another machine's numbers would assert cross-machine comparability we reject. |
    20	| D12 | Set types: warmup/working/failure; warmups excluded from PRs/volume | The minimal set that keeps records honest. RPE/supersets deferred. (Amended by D26: drop sets added.) |
    21	| D13 | Rest timer: auto-start on completion, per-exercise durations (warmup vs. working split), notification | Strong-standard behavior; the split is a verified Strong nicety worth matching. |
    22	| D14 | Load types: weighted / bodyweight / bodyweightPlus / assisted, direction-aware | Assisted machines invert "better" (less assistance wins); naive weight×reps produces wrong PRs. |
    23	| D15 | Strength-only v1 (no duration/distance sets) | Single set shape is the biggest scope containment available. |
    24	| D16 | v1 extras all in scope but sequenced: export → charts → Strong import → plate calc | Everything ships in v1, but a gym-usable build exists early (dogfooding from milestone 2). |
    25	| D17 | e1RM: Brzycki, 12-rep record cap | Strong parity (verified from their docs); estimates degrade past 12 reps. |
    26	| D18 | Adopt Strong's template-drift prompt (update/values-only/both/keep) | Elegant, proven solution to template drift. |
    27	| D19 | Equipment context lives on ExerciseEntry and freezes once the entry's first set completes — permanently, even if that completion is later undone. Switching equipment starts a new entry; uncompleted draft rows move to it, completed sets stay | Prevents completed sets being silently relabeled; keeps snapshots, prefill, and machine PRs historically honest (2026-08-08 Codex cross-review, finding 2). |
    28	| D20 | e1RM is computed for `weighted` exercises only. Assisted records = least assistance per rep count; bodyweight+added = most added weight per rep count (athlete bodyweight intentionally ignored); plain bodyweight = most reps, uncapped. The D17 12-rep cap applies only to weight-keyed rep-count tables | Brzycki is mathematically invalid on inverted (assisted) or offset (bodyweight) loads (review finding 5). |
    29	| D21 | Volume = Σ(normalizedKg × reps) over completed working+failure sets of `weighted` exercises. Dumbbells are logged as labeled (per hand) and never auto-doubled. Assisted/bodyweight excluded from volume in v1 | One consistent honest rule; resolves the deferred dumbbell question; avoids Strong's per-platform divergence (finding 14). |
    30	| D22 | Rest-duration precedence: per-exercise override → global defaults (2:00 working / 1:00 warmup). Failure sets use the working duration. TemplateItem carries no rest duration in v1 | Single storage owner; documents the warmup default explicitly (finding 16). |
    31	| D23 | Context snapshots store stable UUIDs (exercise, machine, model, gym), the exercise's **loadType**, and the **freeWeightTag**, alongside display strings; historical queries (prefill, layers, records) group by snapshot values, never live relationships | A "future-only" model correction or a catalog loadType edit must not reinterpret old sets (findings 3; pass-2 criticals 2–3). |
    32	| D24 | Catalog seeding is versioned, idempotent reconciliation keyed by fixed catalog UUIDs. Mutable seeded fields are allowlisted (name, exercise links, metadata); seeded rows are not user-editable — only user-created models/exercises can be renamed | First-launch-only seeding would strand installs; unrestricted edits would fight reconciliation (finding 9; pass-2 important 8). |
    33	| D26 | **Drop sets are a fourth set type** (marker `D`), reopening D12's deferral at the user's request (2026-08-10). Semantics: a drop set is real work, so it counts toward records and volume exactly like a working or failure set — only warmups are excluded. Because a drop set is *by definition* performed without resting, completing one does **not** auto-start the rest timer (D13/D22 unchanged for the other three types). Prefill's type-aware index matching treats drop as its own sequence, consistent with how warmup and working already match. | The user logs drop sets in real training, so the app was silently unable to describe their actual workouts. Counting them toward records is the honest reading — the weight was lifted. Suppressing the timer encodes what makes a drop set a drop set, rather than nagging with a countdown the user will always skip. |
    34	| D25 | Unit conversion uses exactly 1 lb = 0.45359237 kg; `normalizedKg` recomputes atomically on any value/unit edit; storage keeps full precision. Display: up to 2 decimals, trailing zeros trimmed, half-up rounding, locale-independent decimal point in storage; converted values prefixed ≈ | Executable contract so normalized values can never drift from as-entered values (finding 12). |
    35	| D27 | **Refines D24**: a user-created exercise may be linked to a *seeded* equipment model, and reconciliation **merges** rather than overwrites — a seeded model's `exerciseIDs` becomes the catalog's links (in catalog order) plus the user-added links to `isSeeded == false` exercises the store already held. Stale links to seeded exercises the catalog dropped are still removed. | Ticket 19 lets an unlisted movement be invented at the station it is performed on, and that station is usually a catalog model. `exerciseIDs` is an allowlisted seeded field, so a plain overwrite would silently unlink the user's exercise on the next catalog bump — D24 says reconciliation never touches user-created rows, and dropping a user's link is touching it. |
    36	| D28 | **The export carries the user's data, not the shipped catalog.** Seeded exercises and equipment models appear only where the user's data references them (a machine's model, a history snapshot, a template item, a gym memory, a rest override); references are not followed transitively. The file records `seededCatalogVersion` so the omitted rows stay reproducible from the app itself. | A personal backup that is 1877 rows of someone else's catalog and one workout is not a backup of the user's training. The catalog ships in the binary and is versioned (D24); the user's history exists nowhere else. |
    37	| D29 | **Exports carry weights as entered *and* normalized, never converted for display**: `weight`, `unit`, `weightKg`, at full precision. No `≈` value ever reaches a file. | The ≈ marker is a UI honesty device for a *converted* number (D9/D25). A file has room for both exact numbers, so it gets both — and a consumer never has to guess what unit a column is in, which is exactly Strong's failure (SPEC line 88). |
    38	| D30 | **Nothing is silently dropped from an export.** Uncompleted draft sets, entries with no completed sets, and the workout still in progress all export, flagged (`completed`, empty `finishedAt`). Consumers filter as `RecordsMath` does. | A backup that omits rows is not a backup; the omission is discovered when the data is needed. Flagging costs one column and keeps the file a faithful copy rather than an interpretation. |
    39	| D31 | **Export timestamps are ISO 8601 with the exporting device's UTC offset** (`2026-08-11T18:30:00+09:00`), including in filenames. | A workout log is a record of local time-of-day; bare UTC would file a 6 a.m. Seoul session under the previous evening. The offset preserves both the instant and the wall clock. |
    40	| D32 | **CSV is RFC 4180 with CRLF and a UTF-8 BOM, and user text is written verbatim.** Fields containing quote/comma/CR/LF are quoted with inner quotes doubled; nothing is prefixed to defuse spreadsheet formulas. | The BOM is for Excel, which otherwise mojibakes non-ASCII gym names. Verbatim because the file's whole job is fidelity: rewriting a note that happens to start with `=` corrupts the user's own words to defend them from their own spreadsheet. |
    41	
    42	## Technical
    43	
    44	| # | Decision | Why |
    45	|---|---|---|
    46	| T1 | SwiftData | First-party, SwiftUI-native, fits no-dependency rule. Weak migrations mitigated by simple models + denormalized snapshots. |
    47	| T2 | CloudKit-compatible schema; ship single-device | UUIDs/optional relationships/no unique constraints cost little now; retrofitting sync later is a painful migration. |
    48	| T3 | UI-first build order | User requirement: see and approve the screens (sample data) before logic/persistence is wired. |
    49	| T4 | Xcode buildable folders (synchronized groups) | Adding files doesn't touch project.pbxproj — no merge hazard, and agents can add source files without editing project config. |
    50	| T5 | Repo is the source of truth for spec/context | Any agent session (Claude Code or Codex, either dev) loads identical context from docs/. |
    51	| T6 | Cross-review over double-build | Work is reviewed by a **different agent than the one that wrote it** (Claude ↔ Codex), against the tickets and this decision log. Solo development means this is the only independent check that exists — it is not optional ceremony. Proven 2026-08-08: a Codex review of Codex-written code plus a Claude review of it caught 9 real defects, 3 of which broke the product's historical-honesty thesis. Codex may also independently author adversarial tests for the pure-logic core against the spec. |
    52	| T7 | Unit-default precedence: machine → gym → app preference | Most specific context wins. |
    53	
    54	## Deferred / open
