 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Milestone 3 — CSV / JSON export
     2	
     3	Written 2026-08-11. Closes the `docs/DECISIONS.md` open item "Export column layout / JSON
     4	schema versioning — milestone 3".
     5	
     6	## Why now
     7	
     8	The user's entire training history exists in exactly one place: the SwiftData store on one
     9	iPhone, signed with a certificate that expires every 7 days. There is no sync, no backup, and
    10	no way to read the data off the device. Export is the backup story (SPEC "Technical
    11	direction") and the data-ownership promise (D5: stable IDs + export keep public release open).
    12	
    13	## Two exports, two jobs
    14	
    15	| | CSV | JSON |
    16	|---|---|---|
    17	| Job | read it, chart it, keep it in a spreadsheet | restore it, re-import it, hand it to another tool |
    18	| Shape | one row per set, fully denormalized | the object graph, versioned |
    19	| Audience | a human with Numbers/Excel | a program (including a future importer) |
    20	| Fidelity | every logged set with its full equipment context | everything the user created |
    21	
    22	Neither is lossy in the dimensions SPEC calls out for our export (line 88): **explicit units,
    23	stable UUIDs, set types, full equipment context** — the four things Strong's CSV lacks.
    24	
    25	## Decisions this milestone makes
    26	
    27	Proposed as **D28–D32**, to be recorded in `docs/DECISIONS.md` when ticket 01 lands.
    28	
    29	- **D28 — Export contains the user's data, not the shipped catalog.** Seeded exercises and
    30	  equipment models appear only where the user's data references them (a machine's model, a
    31	  history snapshot, a template item, a gym-exercise memory, a rest override); the export
    32	  records `seededCatalogVersion` so the rest is reproducible from the app itself. Exporting all
    33	  1877 seeded models would make a personal backup mostly someone else's catalog.
    34	- **D29 — Weights export as entered *and* normalized, never converted for display.** Three
    35	  columns/fields: `weight`, `unit`, `weightKg`. No ≈ values anywhere in an export — the ≈ marker
    36	  is a UI honesty device (D9/D25); a file gets both exact numbers instead.
    37	- **D30 — Nothing is silently dropped.** Uncompleted (draft) sets, zero-set entries and the
    38	  in-progress workout all export, flagged (`completed`, `finishedAt` empty). A backup that
    39	  omits rows is not a backup. Consumers filter on `completed` exactly as `RecordsMath` does.
    40	- **D31 — Timestamps are ISO 8601 with the device's UTC offset** (`2026-08-11T18:30:00+09:00`),
    41	  never bare UTC. A workout log is a record of local time-of-day; the offset keeps both the
    42	  instant and the wall clock.
    43	- **D32 — CSV is RFC 4180 with a UTF-8 BOM, and user text is exported verbatim.** CRLF line
    44	  endings, quotes doubled inside quoted fields. The BOM is there because the gym and exercise
    45	  names in this store are not all ASCII and Excel mojibakes UTF-8 without it. Verbatim means no
    46	  formula-injection prefixing: mangling the user's own notes to defend them from themselves is
    47	  the wrong trade for a personal backup.
    48	
    49	## CSV format
    50	
    51	`workout-tracker-YYYY-MM-DD-HHmm.csv`, one header row, one row per `SetRecord`.
    52	
    53	Rows are ordered by workout `startedAt` (ascending, ties broken by workout id), then entry
    54	`order`, then set `order` — deterministic, so two exports of unchanged data are byte-identical.
    55	
    56	All context columns read the **entry's D23 snapshot**, never the live rows: a renamed gym must
    57	not rewrite last month's rows. Empty fields are empty (no `null`, no `-`).
    58	
    59	| # | Column | Source | Notes |
    60	|---|---|---|---|
    61	| 1 | `workoutID` | `Workout.id` | groups rows into a session |
    62	| 2 | `workoutStartedAt` | `Workout.startedAt` | D31 |
    63	| 3 | `workoutFinishedAt` | `Workout.finishedAt` | empty = still active (D30) |
    64	| 4 | `workoutName` | `Workout.sourceTemplateName` | empty when not from a template |
    65	| 5 | `workoutNotes` | `Workout.notes` | |
    66	| 6 | `gymID` | `ExerciseEntry.snapshotGymID` | empty = no gym |
    67	| 7 | `gymName` | `ExerciseEntry.snapshotGymName` | |
    68	| 8 | `entryID` | `ExerciseEntry.id` | |
    69	| 9 | `entryOrder` | `ExerciseEntry.order` | |
    70	| 10 | `exerciseID` | `snapshotExerciseID` | stable UUID |
    71	| 11 | `exerciseName` | `snapshotExerciseName` | |
    72	| 12 | `loadType` | `snapshotLoadType` | raw value — direction-aware records depend on it |
    73	| 13 | `equipmentTag` | `snapshotFreeWeightTag` | empty when the entry used a machine |
    74	| 14 | `machineID` | `snapshotMachineID` | |
    75	| 15 | `machineLabel` | `snapshotMachineLabel` | |
    76	| 16 | `modelID` | `snapshotModelID` | the catalog UUID history keys on |
    77	| 17 | `manufacturer` | resolved from `modelID` | live catalog lookup, display only |
    78	| 18 | `modelName` | resolved from `modelID` | |
    79	| 19 | `setID` | `SetRecord.id` | |
    80	| 20 | `setOrder` | `SetRecord.order` | |
    81	| 21 | `setType` | `SetRecord.type` | `warmup`/`working`/`failure`/`drop` |
    82	| 22 | `reps` | `SetRecord.reps` | empty while draft |
    83	| 23 | `weight` | `SetRecord.weightValue` | as entered, full precision, `.` separator |
    84	| 24 | `unit` | `SetRecord.weightUnit` | D29 — the column Strong has no answer for |
    85	| 25 | `weightKg` | `SetRecord.normalizedKg` | D29 |
    86	| 26 | `completed` | `completedAt != nil` | `true`/`false` |
    87	| 27 | `completedAt` | `SetRecord.completedAt` | |
    88	
    89	Columns 17–18 are the one place the export reads a live row rather than a snapshot: the
    90	snapshot stores `snapshotModelID` and `snapshotModelName` but not the manufacturer. Export the
    91	snapshot's model name and resolve the manufacturer from the catalog, empty if the model is gone.
    92	
    93	## JSON format
    94	
    95	`workout-tracker-YYYY-MM-DD-HHmm.json`, UTF-8, pretty-printed with sorted keys (diffable,
    96	deterministic).
    97	
    98	```jsonc
    99	{
   100	  "schemaVersion": 1,             // bumped on any breaking shape change
   101	  "exportedAt": "2026-08-11T18:30:00+09:00",
   102	  "appVersion": "1.0 (3)",
   103	  "seededCatalogVersion": 4,      // D28: what the omitted catalog rows came from
   104	  "counts": { "workouts": 87, "sets": 1234, ... },
   105	  "preferences": { ... },         // canonical AppPreferences row
   106	  "gyms": [...],                  // incl. archived
   107	  "machines": [...],              // incl. archived, with gymID + modelID
   108	  "exercises": [...],             // referenced or user-created (D28)
   109	  "equipmentModels": [...],       // referenced or user-created (D28)
   110	  "templates": [...],             // with ordered items
   111	  "workouts": [                   // with nested entries → sets, ordered
   112	    { "id": ..., "entries": [ { "snapshot": {...}, "sets": [...] } ] }
   113	  ],
   114	  "gymExerciseMemory": [...],
   115	  "exerciseRestOverrides": [...]
   116	}
   117	```
   118	
   119	Every id is the stable UUID. Every optional that is nil is **omitted**, not `null`. Set
   120	weights carry `weight`, `unit`, `weightKg` (D29). Derived data (PRs, volume, e1RM) is **not**
   121	exported — SPEC: PRs are derived, never source-of-truth.
   122	
   123	## Out of scope
   124	
   125	- Import / restore (JSON is written to be re-importable; reading it back is not this milestone).
   126	- Strong CSV import — milestone 5.
   127	- Automatic/scheduled backup, iCloud Drive sync, encryption.
   128	- Selective export (date ranges, single workout). Everything, every time.
   129	
   130	## Tickets
   131	
   132	```
   133	01 export-core   — value types, collector, JSON + CSV encoders (pure, unit-tested)
   134	└── 02 export-ui — Settings section, share sheet, file naming, counts summary
   135	    └── 03 export-verification — fidelity tests over a realistic store + UI test
   136	```

